{"name":"Sterling","hex":"#d1d4d1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Sterling","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A Scottish surname, variant of Stirling.","An English surname, thought to be a variant of Starling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Sterling"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pound sterling","blocks":[{"text":"Sterling is the currency of the United Kingdom and nine of its dependent territories and formerly of the Kingdom of England. The pound is the base unit of sterling, and the word pound is also used to refer to the British currency generally, often qualified in international contexts as the British pound or pound sterling. Since 1971, each pound has been divided into 100 pence."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c0/British_12_sided_pound_coin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pound_sterling","wikidata":"Q25224"},{"title":"Penny (English coin)","blocks":[{"text":"The English penny, originally a coin of 1.3 to 1.5 grams pure silver, was introduced c. 785 by King Offa of Mercia. These coins were similar in size and weight to the continental deniers of the period and to the Anglo-Saxon sceats which had preceded it."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Edgar%2C_King_of_England%2C_silver_%27reform%27_penny%3B_struck_973-975_AD_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penny_(English_coin)","wikidata":"Q16932458"},{"title":"Sterling silver","blocks":[{"text":"Sterling silver is an alloy composed by weight of 92.5% silver and 7.5% other metals, usually copper. The sterling silver standard has a minimum millesimal fineness of 925."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/TiffanyPitcher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","li